# Fix Prompt for Failing Test /playwright/playwright:<startup>

## Root Cause

The test subject is the Playwright **`<startup>`** pseudo-test, i.e. the `webServer` block in `tests/playwright.config.ts` never came up, so no spec could run.

- `tests/playwright.config.ts` starts the server with `docker compose up --build frontend` from the repo root and waits on `http://127.0.0.1:${port}`.
- The repo-root `docker-compose.yaml` declares `frontend` with `build.context: ./services/frontend` and maps `"${FRONTEND_PORT:-3000}:3000"` (so the app must listen on **0.0.0.0:3000 inside the container**).
- **`services/` does not exist at all.** There is no `services/frontend` directory, so `docker compose up --build frontend` fails immediately with an unreadable build context and startup times out.
- The only spec, `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts`, then asserts `page.goto('/')` renders a single `h1` whose text is exactly `Hello world`.

The fix is therefore to scaffold the missing production service: a minimal Next.js (App Router) + React + TypeScript app at `services/frontend/`, containerized so `docker compose up --build frontend` serves `/` on port 3000 with `<h1>Hello world</h1>`.

`docker-compose.yaml` at the repo root is already correct — **do not touch it**, and do not touch anything under `tests/` or `docs/`. Every file below is new and lives under `services/frontend/`.

